g volumes and ticket types so  coda basically what does it do it takes  the incoming support ticket and then  understands what the intent of the  customer is it uses a lot of the meta  information as well then provides  suggested contact types actions as well  the three most relevant reply templates  to the customer support agent and then  they can choose to use these or not  so let's look delve a little bit more  deeply into the models that we were  using for this customer service  representative I will be starting off  outlining the traditional approaches  that we use and then subsequently we're  going to delve into the deep learning  approaches so for the first iteration of  coda we built a traditional NLP pipeline  in combination with a random forest we  used for different types of feature  feature types here ranging from the user  information to trip information metadata  around the tickets as well as the ticket  message itself now using these features  can be very advantageous because it  reduces greatly the potential reply and  contact type space to give you a  concrete example if we know that a  ticket is coming from a driver partner  we can immediately exclude any issue  types that would be submitted from  writers for example now unsurprisingly  the incoming message from the user  itself is being found to be the most  important feature within within this  model and so in this case we used a  traditional NLP pipeline which consisted  of pre-processing such as tokenization  or stop word removal to yield a bag of  words and then we utilized topic  modeling on top of this bag of words in  order to extract the intent of the  customer now here we use two different  approaches we use tf-idf for a low level  and opie feature extraction and latent  semantic analysis for a high level  feature extraction now we then take all  of these features and pass them through  a multi-class classification algorithm  in this case a random forest to yield  the top three contact types as well as  top three replied templates framing this  as a multi-class classification problem  seemed the first natural thing to do but  what we realized is the model accuracy  was not quite where we wanted it to be  so therefore  we pivoted the approach to a binary  classification with point-wise ranking  methodology and we also introduced class  prototypes for each specific reply  template as well as contact type a  specimen so to say that we got from  historic data we then used the output  and the score from the binary  classification for the ranking again to  obtain the top three contact types and  top three reply templates now this  approach was 25 percent more accurate  than the multi-class classification  approach an addition also provided much  faster training times  so given this success we then went ahead  productionize this model and rent some a  B tests and we were very excited to see  an eight point eight percent reduction  in the average handle time which  directly means that the customer can get  their tickets resolved much faster and  very very importantly we didn't see any  reduction in the customer satisfaction  score by deploying this model now this  model is live for multiple different  languages including English Spanish and  Portuguese and covers over 90% of our  ticket volume now as I mentioned earlier  deep learning methodologies have been  very dominant in the natural language  and conversational AI space and so we  want to also test whether deep learning  approaches could be beneficial to this  customer obsession ticket assistant to  this end we evaluated six different text  encoding models these differ by two  major things the first thing whether we  use word or character based approaches  now character based approaches can be  very good if you have misspellings or  out of vocabulary words we also had both  character convolutional neural networks  or recurrent neural networks as well  across these six approaches and as you  can see on 