order to do this  we use the unsupervised learning  methodology dok2 Veck this is an  expansion of word Tuvok where Tuvok  basically allows you to come up with  word embeddings dr. Vivek allows you to  come up with things like sentence or  paragraph document and  when you employ it so we basically use  millions of English uber chat data and  they basically ran it through the dr.  vex model now let me show you what this  looks like here we see at isni plot of  some of these sentence embeddings that  we got as you can see we get some really  really nice clusters here of various  different topics that writers and  drivers are conversing about that our  data scientists then labeled so this  brings me to the next challenge which is  obviously that these clusters are come  out unlabeled in nature and so we  basically hand labeled a couple of  thousand examples too then basically  determine what these clusters actually  mean and what the topics of conversation  are the rest is pretty straightforward  we calculate centroids of each of these  intent clusters and then this concludes  the training phase whenever we have an  incoming message done at run time we  calculate the distance between this  message and the intent cluster centroid  and then we assign it to the nearest  cluster so with this we basically  conclude the intent detection and  discovery phase of this project and as I  mentioned in the introduction the reply  retrieval is merely a lookup of  potential responses that have been  predetermined so now let's go back to  the original example the real-world  example that I showed you  i'm washington for you and actually when  we utilize this approach they're just  shooting the previous slides it  correctly identifies that Washington  should be waiting and so there is a big  advantage that we have from actually  using our data and building the sentence  embeddings ourselves as it can be can  pick up the context within our ecosystem  so next I want to give you a glimpse of  what the future looks like of one click  chat we obviously want to not only  provide text modalities but also whose  new message from musi I'm waiting by the  Starbucks do you want me to respond tell  you see I'll be right there  ok sure ready to send it send it  great message then so these new features  that we're building will allow driver  partners to also provide answers to the  writers using their voice which is very  exciting and in my final part I want to  tell you a little bit about the building  blocks that many conversational AI  systems have in common and as I  mentioned in the introduction the  combination of having a large number of  use cases at uber for natural language  and conversational AI with the fact that  a lot of this is abstract about really  lends itself well to platform ization so  in the previous examples we have seen  that we really need to understand the  intent so basically the topic of  conversation of the end user and so this  is typically handled by a semantic  parser on this front now so far we have  only seen single turn conversations so  one incoming message one response you  would probably want to evolve this to a  multi turn conversation with a back and  forth and here the dialog manager comes  into play who for example tracks the  state of the conversation and then  finally we want to move from a semantic  representation which is not human  understandable back into something that  can be consumed by a human being and  here the language generator comes into  play we are actively building such a  natural language conversational AI  platform at uber and we're actually  using some of the latest cutting-edge  technologies and not only that we're  actively working with our counterparts  in our AI labs or research division to  push the envelope on these methodologies  as well and so if you want to learn more  whether it's about the applications that  I've shown you today or about the new  developments that we're doing in this  space we have several blog posts on our  uber inch blog we have a recent paper at  kdd and we have two submissions for nips  where we're also going to talk about  some of the multi-turn conversationally  I  and devar's that we have been starting  and with this I would like to thank you  very much for your attention and if you  have any questions and happy to discuss  them over lunch thank you very much  [Applause]
 

